        "title": "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "5706e08a-8a9b-5357-a5fc-b39f755d0330",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} 4 \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\csc{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi}{12}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} 4 \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\csc{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=2\\left(\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\cot{\\left(u \\right)} \\csc{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cot{\\left(u \\right)} \\csc{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=- \\csc{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nEndpoint values: $\\csc{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}=2,\\quad \\csc{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}=1$.\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[- 2 \\csc{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}=\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(-4\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n2.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "1d79769f-4b26-543f-825e-ac05959c0c59",
