      "question": {
        ":question/id": "f9ef0223-5ac0-581e-a037-c22f02804cd5",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 3 \\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 3 \\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\int \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\sec{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\int \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{3 \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
