    {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186068916,
        "id": "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6",
        "title": "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e1c02b6f-9b28-5b6e-9b46-7cfdd9245782",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{8}}^{0} 4 \\sec^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=- \\frac{\\pi}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\\\x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{8}}^{0} 4 \\sec^{2}{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=2\\left(\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0} \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nEndpoint values: $\\tan{\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}=-1,\\quad \\tan{\\left(0 \\right)}=0$.\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[2 \\tan{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(-2\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n2.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
