      "question": {
        ":question/id": "20dc1f0e-e8bc-5bb4-b605-c55b3ca0b2ef",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 4 \\sec^{2}{\\left(4 x + 3 \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=4 x + 3$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 4 \\sec^{2}{\\left(4 x + 3 \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\tan{\\left(4 x + 3 \\right)}+C.\n$$",
