      "question": {
        ":question/id": "fb55166d-e7d5-5d79-9dfc-21d2e9f48bda",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 4 \\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 4 \\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=2\\left(\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\sec{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nEndpoint values: $\\sec{\\left(0 \\right)}=1,\\quad \\sec{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right)}=2$.\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[2 \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(2\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n2.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "0b336269-20d1-5e77-b894-50bc84329d69",
