      "question": {
        ":question/id": "251d2494-5d7a-591d-8549-96f9e9775e7d",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} 2 \\sec^{2}{\\left(- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}&\\Longrightarrow u=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new bounds decrease. Keep this order when evaluating the antiderivative.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} 2 \\sec^{2}{\\left(- 2 x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=-\\left(\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\tan{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nEndpoint values: $\\tan{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}=1,\\quad \\tan{\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}=-1$.\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[- \\tan{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(-1\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n2.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
