        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 2 \\tan{\\left(- 2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(- 2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=- 2 x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=0\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}&\\Longrightarrow u=- \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new bounds decrease. Keep this order when evaluating the antiderivative.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 2 \\tan{\\left(- 2 x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(- 2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=-\\left(\\int_{0}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{\\left(u \\right)} \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\sec{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nEndpoint values: $\\sec{\\left(0 \\right)}=1,\\quad \\sec{\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right)}=2$.\n\nIntegrate and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\left[- \\sec{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}}=\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(-1\\right).\n$$\n\nThe answer is\n\n$$\n-1.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
